<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beemster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1612</span> · Also known as Beemster Polder</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Beemster is a polder in North Holland, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, created by draining a lake between 1609 and 1612 with dozens of windmills under the direction of Jan Leeghwater, with money from Amsterdam merchants. Its fields, roads and canals were laid out in a regular grid that became a model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> land reclamation. It was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1999.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beemster.jpg" alt="Beemster" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arch · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Midden_beemster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
